WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:00.750
Node.

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:04.863
js applications sometimes call external processes for image processing, document conversion,

00:00:04.863 --> 00:00:07.481
system utilities, build steps, or operational scripts.

00:00:07.481 --> 00:00:11.594
Command injection risk appears when user-controlled input influences the command, arguments,

00:00:11.594 --> 00:00:15.707
options, environment, file paths, or working directory in an unsafe way.

00:00:15.707 --> 00:00:20.194
The danger increases when code builds one shell string and asks the

00:00:20.194 --> 00:00:22.064
operating system to interpret it.

00:00:22.064 --> 00:00:26.925
A safer design starts by asking whether a shell is needed at all.

00:00:26.925 --> 00:00:30.291
Many tasks can be performed through libraries, platform APIs,

00:00:30.291 --> 00:00:35.152
or process execution modes that pass a fixed executable and controlled arguments separately.

00:00:35.152 --> 00:00:38.517
Avoiding shell interpretation removes a large class of ambiguity.

00:00:38.517 --> 00:00:40.387
When external execution is necessary,

00:00:40.387 --> 00:00:44.126
the set of allowed operations should be narrow and explicit.

00:00:44.126 --> 00:00:45.248
Argument handling matters.

00:00:45.248 --> 00:00:48.613
User choices should map to allowlisted operations, file types,

00:00:48.613 --> 00:00:51.605
or options rather than becoming free-form command text.

00:00:51.605 --> 00:00:54.970
File paths should be resolved carefully within intended directories.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:56.840
Environment variables should be controlled.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.327
The process should run with the least privilege practical for the task,

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:05.066
and the working directory should not be left to chance.

00:01:05.066 --> 00:01:08.058
Logging should support investigation without leaking sensitive data.

00:01:08.058 --> 00:01:12.919
It is useful to record which approved operation was requested, who requested it,

00:01:12.919 --> 00:01:14.415
and whether it succeeded.

00:01:14.415 --> 00:01:19.276
It is not useful to dump secrets, full command strings containing private values,

00:01:19.276 --> 00:01:21.893
or untrusted input into logs without thought.

00:01:21.893 --> 00:01:24.511
Observability and privacy need to be balanced.

00:01:24.511 --> 00:01:28.998
The defensive principle is to avoid string-built commands, separate commands from arguments,

00:01:28.998 --> 00:01:32.737
constrain what users can request, prefer safer libraries when practical,

00:01:32.737 --> 00:01:35.729
and treat process execution as a sensitive sink.

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:39.940
The more powerful the command environment, the stricter the design should be.
